<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yajnavalkya</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yajnavalkya was a Vedic sage of Videha, regarded as the teacher of the White <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yajurveda">Yajurveda</a> and the source of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatapatha-brahmana">Shatapatha Brahmana</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brihadaranyaka-upanishad">Brihadaranyaka Upanishad</a> he wins the philosophical contest at King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janaka">Janaka</a>'s court and teaches that the self, atman, is identical with brahman. A later law code, the Yajnavalkya Smriti, was attributed to him and was commented on by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijnaneshvara">Vijnaneshvara</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yajnavalkya.jpg" alt="Yajnavalkya" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: எஸ். பி. கிருஷ்ணமூர்த்தி · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yajnavalkya_and_Janaka.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yajnavalkya, whose name derives from yajna, 'ritual', is presented as a pupil of Vaisampayana and of Uddālaka Āruṇi, and as the compiler of the Shukla Yajurveda Samhita. The Taittiriya Upanishad tells that Vaisampayana grew angry with him, one of his leading disciples, and demanded back all the knowledge he had taught him. Yajnavalkya vomited it up, and the guru told his other disciples to take the form of partridges, taittiriya birds, and consume it, which gave the Upanishad its name. The story is meant to show his genius, and he is also said to have added his own insight, which pleased the guru. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brihadaranyaka-upanishad">Brihadaranyaka Upanishad</a>, dated to about 700 BCE, he is depicted as blunt, provocative and sarcastic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Yajnavalkya stands at the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brihadaranyaka-upanishad">Brihadaranyaka Upanishad</a>. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janaka">Janaka</a> of Videha, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithila">Mithila</a>, held a sacrifice called the Bahudakshina Yajna, at which he offered a thousand cows with gold horns to the best brahmin. Yajnavalkya at once told his disciple Samasrava to drive the cows home. When the other sages, led by Asvala, protested, he replied that he did not claim to be the best brahmin, and had simply wanted the cows. A debate followed in which he answered each challenger, among them Uddalaka Aruni, Bhujyu, Sakalya, Artabhaga, Ushasta and Kahola.</p><p>The scholar Gargi questioned him twice. In section 3.6 he told her not to question too much, lest her head fall off, when she asked about the support of Brahman. In 3.8 she asked what supports space, and he spoke of the imperishable, the seer who is not seen. She then declared that no other brahmin could defeat him. In chapter 4 he decides to renounce the world and leave his family, and his wife Maitreyi, a discusser of Brahman, asks him about the soul and declines wealth that cannot make her immortal. His other wife, Katyayani, is described as knowing the matters of women.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>He is associated with the Shukla Yajurveda, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatapatha-brahmana">Shatapatha Brahmana</a>, the two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharma</a> text Yājñavalkya Smṛti, which sets out conduct, legal procedure and atonement, and was widely known through the commentary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitakshara">Mitakshara</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijnaneshvara">Vijnaneshvara</a> in the mid-twelfth century. The Yoga Yajnavalkya was probably written many centuries later by someone else.</p><p>His teaching describes the self by negation, 'not this, not this' (neti neti), and holds that love is love for the self, ātman. It contains one of the earliest expositions of karma and rebirth: a person becomes good by good acts and bad by bad acts. Witzel credits him with coining the term Advaita, and his ideas on renunciation have been important to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> sannyasa traditions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yajnavalkya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Someshvara II, introduced the Chalukya-Vikrama era to date records from his accession and presided over a court that included the Kashmiri poet Bilhana, author of the Vikramankadevacharita, and the jurist Vijnaneshvara, whose Mitakshara commentary on the law code of <b>Yajnavalkya</b> became a leading authority on Hindu inheritance law.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The early teachings are attributed to sages such as <b>Yajnavalkya</b>, Uddalaka Aruni, Shvetaketu, Shandilya, Aitareya, Balaki, Pippalada and Sanatkumara, and women such as Maitreyi and Gargi take part in the dialogues.</blockquote><a href="/work/upanishads">Upanishads · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Brihadaranyaka Upanishad King Janaka hosts contests between Brahmin teachers, and the sage <b>Yajnavalkya</b> expounds the doctrine of the self, atman, and its identity with brahman.</blockquote><a href="/state/videha">Videha</a></li></ul></section>
